//! `dx.op.*` function declarations and the `dx.types.*` structs (owner: the bitcode subagent).
//!
//! The contract the lowering code is written against:
//! * [`call`] appends `call @dx.op.<class>[.<overload>](i32 <opcode>, args...)`, declaring the
//!   function on first use with the signature and attributes DXC gives it (hctdb),
//! * the `dx.types` accessors return the named struct types, declared on first use.
//!
//! The table below is DXC's `OP::OpCodeProps` (name, class, overload mask, attribute) joined
//! with the parameter lists of `OP::GetOpFunc`, generated by `tools/gen_dxop.py` from
//! lib/DXIL/DxilOperations.cpp (v1.9.2609). Every core opcode with a scalar signature is
//! there (all of DXIL 1.0 to 1.7); node, hit-object and vector operations have an empty
//! signature and are [`Error::Unsupported`].
//!
//! `!dx.precise`: under `-Gis` DXC marks exactly the dx.op calls whose return type is half,
//! float or double (`DxilGenerationPass::TranslatePreciseAttribute`: every user of a dx.op
//! function with `getReturnType()->isFPOrFPVectorTy()`). Calls that return a struct
//! (`ResRet`, `CBufRet`, `SamplePos`, `splitdouble`), an integer or void are not marked.

